CS4254 Project 2: Simple DNS Client
===================================

Build:   make
Run:     ./simplednsClient @<server[:port]> <name>
Tests:   ./test.sh                  (comparison with the reference client)
         ./tests/run_extra_tests.sh (malformed and unusual responses)


High-level approach
-------------------
The client is split into three parts:

  src/query.c       Builds the query. createQNAME() turns the name into DNS
                    labels (length byte + characters, ending in a zero byte),
                    and createDnsRequest() writes the 12-byte header (ID 0x0539,
                    recursion desired, one question) followed by the question
                    (QTYPE A, QCLASS IN).

  src/response.c    Validates and interprets the reply. readName() decodes
                    names, including compression pointers. parseDnsResponse()
                    checks the header, the question and every resource record
                    before anything is printed.

  simplednsClient.c Parses the command line, sends the query over UDP with
                    sendto(), and waits for the reply with poll() and
                    recvfrom() until a 5-second deadline.

All header fields are read and written byte by byte in network order instead
of through a C struct with bit-fields, because bit-field layout depends on the
compiler and the machine's byte order.

The response is walked twice. The first pass checks the whole packet: the
answer, authority and additional sections must all be well formed and must
end exactly at the end of the packet. Only if that pass succeeds does the
second pass print the IP and CNAME lines. A malformed packet therefore
produces a single ERROR line and never a partial answer.

Output rules:
  - A record      ->  IP<tab>address<tab>auth|nonauth
  - CNAME record  ->  CNAME<tab>alias<tab>auth|nonauth
  - auth/nonauth comes from the AA bit in the response header
  - NXDOMAIN, or a NOERROR reply with no A/CNAME answers  ->  NOTFOUND
  - no valid reply within 5 seconds                       ->  NORESPONSE
  - FORMERR, SERVFAIL, NOTIMP, REFUSED, other RCODEs, malformed packets,
    and local failures (bad arguments, socket or send errors)  ->  ERROR<tab>reason


Error handling
--------------
Command line: the server must be @a.b.c.d with an optional port from 1 to
65535 (inet_pton and strtol, with no trailing characters). The name must have
labels of 1-63 bytes and must be at most 255 bytes when encoded; one trailing
dot is allowed. Invalid input produces an ERROR line before anything is sent.

Ignored packets: datagrams from any address or port other than the server's,
datagrams whose ID is not 0x0539, and datagrams without the QR (response) bit
are silently ignored, and the client keeps waiting. The 5-second deadline is
measured on a monotonic clock from when the query is sent, so a stream of
ignored packets cannot extend the wait.

Rejected responses (ERROR):
  - shorter than the 12-byte header
  - a nonzero opcode, or the TC (truncated) bit set
  - a question count other than 1, or a question whose name (compared without
    regard to case), type or class differs from the query
  - a name that runs past the end of the packet, uses a reserved label type,
    is longer than 255 bytes, or has a compression pointer that does not point
    backwards (this rules out pointer loops); jumps are also capped at 64
  - a record whose fixed fields or RDATA run past the end of the packet
  - an A answer whose RDLENGTH is not 4, an A or CNAME answer whose class is
    not IN, or a CNAME whose name does not fill its RDATA exactly
  - extra bytes after the last record

Answer records of other types (for example AAAA) are skipped. Bytes in names
that are not printable, as well as '.' and '\' inside a label, are escaped the
way dig prints them (\. \\ \DDD) so the output stays one line per record.

Buffers: the receive buffer is 64 KB, so a large UDP reply is never cut off,
and all name text is written with explicit length checks. The client runs
cleanly under valgrind and compiles without warnings with -Wall -Wextra.


Challenges
----------
- The starter header described the flags as C bit-fields. Their order in
  memory does not match the order of the bits on the wire, so we replaced
  them with masks on a 16-bit value read in network byte order.
- Name compression: a pointer can lead to more labels that end in another
  pointer, and a malicious packet can form a cycle. Requiring every pointer
  to point backwards, together with the 255-byte name limit, guarantees the
  decoder stops.
- Timeouts: setting SO_RCVTIMEO once would restart the 5-second wait every
  time an unrelated packet arrived, so we track the remaining time and pass
  it to poll() on every iteration.
- Strictness versus partial output: validating the whole packet before
  printing is what keeps malformed responses from printing some answers
  followed by an error.


Testing
-------
1. ./test.sh compares our query dump and parsed answers with the reference
   client on the course test server (172.21.222.45): google.com, www.cnn.com,
   vt.edu, amazon.com, missing.cs4254 (NXDOMAIN), multi.cs4254 (several A
   records, auth), alias.cs4254 (CNAME, auth), error.cs4254 (SERVFAIL) and the
   5-second timeout against 192.0.2.1. All 9 tests pass.

2. tests/run_extra_tests.sh starts tests/fake_dns_server.py, a small UDP
   server on 127.0.0.1 that answers our query with a crafted packet, and checks
   our output for 25 cases: NOERROR with no answers, a packet shorter than the
   header, the TC bit, a wrong ID followed by the real reply, a non-response
   (QR=0) followed by the real reply, a reply from another port followed by the
   real reply, pointer loops, forward pointers, a 5-byte A record, RDLENGTH past
   the end of the packet, trailing bytes, a mismatched question, a question
   count of 0, REFUSED, FORMERR, an unknown RCODE, a CNAME chain using
   compression, AAAA mixed with A, the AA flag, 40 A records (larger than 512
   bytes), names that need escaping, a question echoed in uppercase, an
   NXDOMAIN with an SOA in the authority section, a non-IN class, and no reply
   at all. All 25 pass.

3. Manual checks: invalid server strings, ports 0/70000/"5x", empty names,
   "..", labels over 63 bytes, names over 255 bytes, trailing dots, and
   mixed-case names; output compared with dig @172.21.222.45 <name>; and a
   valgrind run with no errors.
